      "knowledge_point": "Applying the Extended Euclidean Algorithm When the Two Numbers are Not Coprime",
      "kp_id": "e-6763"
    },
    "q-103202": {
      "dom_id": "step-q103202",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "Using the extended Euclidean algorithm, compute integers $u$ and $v$ such that $70u+25v=\\text{gcd}(70,25).$ What is the value of $u+v?$",
      "worked_solution": "We will use the extended Euclidean algorithm. First, we apply the forward reduction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}70 & = & 25\\cdot 2 & + & 20 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 25 & = & 20\\cdot 1 & + & 5 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 20 & = & 5\\cdot 4 & + & 0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, $\\text{gcd}(70,25)=5.$\n\nSolving for the remainders (the rightmost terms) in the equations above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}20 & =70-2\\cdot 25 \\\\ 5 & =25-20.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we back-substitute:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5 & =25-20 \\\\  & =25-(70-2\\cdot 25) \\\\  & =25\\cdot 3-70 \\\\  & =70\\cdot (-1)+25\\cdot 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $u=-1$ and $v=3.$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\nu+v=(-1)+3=2.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
