    "q-77102": {
      "dom_id": "step-q77102",
      "name": "Question 7",
      "problem": "Using the extended Euclidean algorithm, compute integers $u$ and $v$ such that $71u+22v=\\text{gcd}(71,22).$ What is the value of $u+v?$",
      "worked_solution": "We will use the extended Euclidean algorithm. First, we apply the forward reduction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}71 & = & 22\\cdot 3 & + & 5 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 22 & = & 5\\cdot 4 & + & 2 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 5 & = & 2\\cdot 2 & + & 1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, $\\text{gcd}(22,71)=1.$\n\nSolving for the remainders (the rightmost terms) in the equations above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5 & =71-22\\cdot 3 \\\\ 2 & =22-5\\cdot 4 \\\\ 1 & =5-2\\cdot 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we back-substitute:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =5-2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =5-[22-5\\cdot 4]\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =5\\cdot 9-22\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =[71-22\\cdot 3]\\cdot 9-22\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =71\\cdot 9-22\\cdot 29 \\\\  & =71\\cdot 9+22\\cdot (-29)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $u=9$ and $v=-29.$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\nu+v=9+(-29)=-20.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
